[{:seon.task/name "a-merge-writes-back-only-what-it-changed"
  :seon.task/plan "A form present in the source root but absent from the running binary is lost by a merge's write-back. Reproduction: bin/check 100's e2e-loop with target/seon built from main e117b9b and the source tree at a branch adding the private fn `seon.db/stored-id`: the loop's merge commit changed src/seon/fixtures.clj as asked and also deleted `stored-id` from src/seon/db.clj (12 lines), LOOP FAILED; on a binary rebuilt from the same source the loop passes (:files src/seon/fixtures.clj only). A binary older than its source is the normal case after a merge loads in place (docs/source.md#a-merge-loads-in-place), so this can delete merged code. Cause not found: the merge's delta, or the write-back's rendering of the file, treats the form as retracted. Correct: the write-back renders exactly the files whose forms the merge changed, from main's live forms, whatever the binary holds. The proof is not written."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.e2e-test/a-merge-on-an-older-binary-keeps-the-forms-it-lacks}}
 {:seon.task/name "test-namespaces-built-into-the-binary"
  :seon.task/plan "A root importing test/ restarted in 8.7-9.1 s against 3.5-3.6 s without it, and held 119-130 MB against 111 MB (`seon.lib/real-mb`; 382 namespaces against 333), measured with bin/root start at main 9382656 (before the changes below; not re-measured). Cause: the binary (`jolt build -m seon.main`) compiles src only, so a root with test/ in `:seon.config.source/dirs` (default [\\\"src\\\" \\\"test\\\" \\\"resources/seon/schemas\\\" \\\"resources/seon/config\\\" \\\"bin\\\"], resources/seon/config/default.edn, whose comment names this task for the +5 s a restart) evaluates the test forms one by one. What has moved since: the fork server holds the tests' load as due (`:tests-due` in `seon.fork-server`) and loads them at the first fork, not at the boot, and `bin/build` warms the binary's compile cache with the test namespaces (`seon.check/warm-tests`), so an agent does not compile them at its first fork. Still missing: the binary holds the test namespaces as it holds src's, so `seon.program/load-all!` finds them held and loads none. Check first what v1 did and whether Jolt's build takes a second source root (jolt build's :paths, a deps alias :test) without the test runner's main. Proof (not written): a restart of a root importing src and test/ costs no more than 1.2x a src-only restart, measured with `seon.profile/now-ns`. Why it matters (Step E's dry run, 2026-10-22): a worktree root on the default dirs cannot work any task whose proof belongs in an existing test namespace (the design agent could not load seon.e2e-test or seon.test-store)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/a-root-importing-test-restarts-as-fast-as-src}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-trees-commit-merges-three-way"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: when main's merged forms and a worktree session's edits to the same function meet at import, the tree wins: `seon.source/replace-tx` makes main's program the tree's, asked by an index, `seon.root/import-tree!` or `my.root/import!` (docs/source.md#indexing-a-tree). So a form an agent merged since the tree's commit is lost when the tree, unchanged there, is imported. Correct: the import is the tree's program merged into main by `seon.merge/three-way` against their merge base (main's program at the tree commit it last imported or wrote back), so only what the tree changed applies, and a both-sides change is an unmerged entry like any merge's. Deletes replace-tx's whole-program overwrite."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/an-import-keeps-a-form-main-merged-since-the-trees-commit}}
 {:seon.task/name "another-trees-ns-form-analyzes-beside-the-image"
  :seon.task/plan "A second case of the class, 2026-11-05: a branch moved seon.turn/call-problem to seon.repl, and main's test naming it with var made the base tree's analysis throw in the branch's image, failing bin/check health with no record of why; the test was changed to resolve it when run, which works around it. Any form of the base naming a var the branch moved does this. What is wanted: analyzing another tree's form never fails the step. Found on jolt/docs-any-md, 2026-11-01: bin/check health (test-runner/health!) analyzes the base commit's program in the image of the branch's (program-db of the base tree, test-runner/keep-tree!, seon.source/refresh-tx), and seon.source/analyze-form runs each ns form in the live namespace. A branch that points an alias at another namespace (seon.problems' [seon.docs :as docs] became [seon.doc :as docs]) made the base's ns form throw IllegalStateException \"Alias docs already exists in namespace seon.problems, aliasing seon.doc\" (Jolt's ns-alias-check!, reference-code/jolt/host/chez/ns.ss), and the whole health step failed. Clearing the namespace's aliases before its ns form (which analyze-form's docstring already promises: the file's aliases, whatever the namespace held) only moved it: the base's ns form requires namespaces that load from the checkout's disk (my.test, new code), which then clash the other way. Worked around by aliasing seon.doc as doc, so no alias name changes its target. The cause: one image analyzes two programs whose namespaces disagree. Done 2026-11-01 (jolt/refresh-skips-removed-dependents, for the context lab's parent-tree dbs): refresh-tx never reads a removed dependent's old text again; a namespace whose file the checkout lacks is marked loaded once analyzed (seon.source/in-checkout?) and read again from db's text with a change; an ns form's alias pointing elsewhere is cleared before it runs (only that alias). Proof seon.source-test/a-renamed-alias-target-analyzes-beside-the-image covers the removed dependent, the off-checkout require both ways and the repointed alias; the re-read of an unchanged off-checkout namespace is shown only by the lab's runner on Seon's pre-rename trees (an in-process test could not reproduce a process that never held it). Left, the cleaner cause: an analysis of another tree that never loads a namespace from disk (its requires answered by the tree's own files, as the analysis reads them, or each tree analyzed in a process of its own), and the proof seon.source-test/a-renamed-alias-target-analyzes-beside-the-image: a base tree aliasing x as a.old and a head aliasing x as a.new both analyze in one process. Checked 2026-11-06: seon.source-test/a-renamed-alias-target-analyzes-beside-the-image is written and green, so the proof named in Left exists; its input renames a namespace (ra.old-lib to ra.new-lib) under an unchanged alias, forward and back. No test covers the 2026-11-05 case (a base form that names a var the branch moved)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/a-renamed-alias-target-analyzes-beside-the-image}}
 {:seon.task/name "seon-reader-is-the-one-program-reader"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-03, on the bug-class audit read at c200a44aa9: \"make sure all of this fixes are applied and the docs are updated\"; by docs/merging.md#a-landing-message-carries-its-audit its report says what allowed the class, why the change makes it impossible, and which siblings it checked. What exists: program text is read by clojure.core/read under a bound *ns* in some places and by seon.reader in others, so ::alias/kw resolution depends on the call site (ece53cd25f moved one site). Still on clojure.core/read: seon.source/read-forms (used by accounts and by the defs map), ns-form, and three `read` calls in seon.repl (two functions; checked 2026-11-06 by grep); seon.config/parse-set and seon.reader swallow the read error and answer a generic message. The change: seon.reader is the one reader of program text; those sites use it; a read error is the reader's own, named; a bin/laws rule lists clojure.core/read and read-string in src as catch is listed. Docs: docs/source.md and docs/failure-points.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/an-unloaded-namespaces-alias-keyword-reads}}
 {:seon.task/name "seon-files-move-by-branch"
  :seon.task/plan "DONE on main: edit! on any path; scripts and config as files of the program (`:file/*`, `[:script path]`, `[:config k]`, seon.fx/unreadable); a process reads config from its own branch; a boot names a file it can't load. A script is the file entity; ``:seon.script/name``, `/text` and `/mode` below were dropped. NEXT: sweep what a running process or a test reads of the checkout's program files (tests' slurps; config_test's was run 8's cause). Real run 9's closures weren't that: schemas are baked at compile, and the cause was root's watcher importing an agent's disk write onto main, then seon.merge/merge-tx throwing on the imported key. Both handled. :schema/source is unused and leaves at the next store erase. LEFT: a branch adding or deleting a script; an unsynced merge meeting a tree edit, where the tree wins today (``seon.source-test/an-import-beside-an-unsynced-merge-is-an-unmerged-entry`` isn't written); a new config key's comment doesn't travel; merged :bind/* keys wait for a restart; a checkout's target/check-store on retired names is refused until removed, and naming it by schema digest would end that. RULINGS, owner, 2026-11-07, over anything below that differs. NO PARALLEL SYSTEM (owner: \"is the indexing a shared system or are we building another parallel system\"): a schema resource is already a file indexed as `:file/path` and `:file/text` with an entity per entry, through seon.source/schema-tx, import-tx, refresh-tx, `[:schema k]` in seon.merge, and changed-files and schema-file-text at write-back. Generalise that to a file of entries; default.edn is the same shape and a script is one entry. `:seon.script/fact` below may only hold what's derived for reading, never its own text. Index default.edn and bin/* now; deps.edn, system.edn and web/ stay ordinary files until something needs them; config/local.edn stays on disk, it is per checkout. A running process reads config from its own branch's db, not the file; agents keep the whole disk and a file they write acts on nothing until an import. Sync happens after each merge that passed its gate, always from main: :seon.config.source/write-back-on-merge stays true and configurable, there's no hand sync to build first, and approval by a person or root is later work. No worktrees and no virtual disk. New checks: before writing a file, check it as its reader reads it (config validates, EDN reads, a form reads) and refuse it otherwise; a boot that can't load what's on disk says which file and which commit last worked, instead of dying. Agreed seams. With Web: `[:config k]` and `[:script path]` are program keys in seon.merge, conflicting per key like a form, carried by `data-attrs #{}`; a branch may hold its own value, which is an ordinary conflict. With Optimization: keep seon.config/resolve-in's signature, its read recording sits on it; don't pull per read (`value` is 1.1 M calls a day's verdicts, `from-db` 285 k ticks and 0.92 MB), hold the map and refresh when a transaction touches a config entity; a fork inherits it; the source is the process's own branch, never whatever db is around, so a test's my.test/world doesn't change what a key reads; keys that locate the store stay in file and env (:seon.config/place is about that set); provide one function from a db to a class value, per config key and for :seon.env/bin, which Optimization calls from seon.agent/branch-inputs, and tell it before merging the config step so its overlay lands with it, or a green is keyed on the wrong input; what's read at load (defs, :bind/* system buffers) keeps the file's value until a write-back and rebuild, so check those keys don't diverge silently from a branch's facts. Sync order: gate on the candidate, write-back only if its content is that candidate, laws and health on the result, commit. Seen and reproduced 2026-11-07, with two agents on one root and nothing merged: one agent's `spit` of a key with no schema into default.edn turned a second agent's live run of seon.config-test/the-shipped-file-validates-under-every-profile red, stopped a new process on that checkout from loading its config (seon.config refuses an unknown key; a root's own restart wasn't tried), and moved no digest, since an agent's digests take the inputs of root's boot: the second agent's prove answered green while its live run was red. No false verdict reached the cache in that run. In deepseek-run7 two tasks ended on config and vector reds that fit this, not traced one by one. So the class isn't a shared disk, which stays: it's that a running system reads its own state from files no merge carries. Do config first: a process takes it from the branch's db, and disk only at the import. GO (owner, 2026-11-07), a first implementation; steps 1 and 2 are the point, the rest follow if they earn it. Why: a task whose work is a Seon file outside `:seon.config.source/dirs` has no route. `roots-log-is-bounded` needed `bin/root` and a config key: its agent wrote them with `spit` to the owner's main tree by absolute path, took the execute bit off, and nothing reached a branch. A `bin/ports` edit shows ` M` in git while `(my.branch/changes)` is `{}`, and nothing says so.

The owner's words, which bound this: \"The DB is the source of truth we need to merge to main cleanly and then we can efficienltly sync to disk if we want. That shouldn't be automated yet have your agents manually call it and verify it's working right.\" \"we have an index step right? we could scan and import more during that time.\" \"an allow list with patterns seems like a good idea.\" \"I don't want to replicate a full filesystem in the db. So we can't ask what's in bin/* in case we only allow listed one file and the agent thinks they are doing an ls. I want clear schemas created that indicate how these files are helpful in some way and yeah I want it pull pattern and render friendly. Don't do any worktrees stuff or virtual file systems.\" \"I like the edit tools working with files.\" \"no refusing writes or trying to do worktrees with seon agents. Seon agents have their own databases which is their worktree.\" \"File ops will be required for other work like editing non seon code or just doing computer maintainance.\"

The design. These aren't files in the db. Each is a kind of thing Seon has, named for what it's for; a file is where its text lives. No directory entity, no parent, no listing, no path tree: an agent reaches a thing by what cites it, by text search, or by a question about that kind (\"what commands does Seon have?\"). `ls` works on the real disk. A file that fits no kind stays an ordinary file: agents read and write anything, as they do now.

A place is a schema, not a protocol: ``:seon.text/place`` {:seon.text/at :seon.text/text}, plain data that any function working on text takes in its contract. What differs by kind is only where text is read from and what a write does, one multimethod at the edge, as `seon.server/op-answer` dispatches:
- a var: its recorded source; evaluates and records it (as `seon.repl/edit!` does today);
- a doc section: its block; loads the page on the branch (as today);
- a kind on the allow-list: its entity; transacts on the branch;
- any other path: the disk; writes the file, keeping its mode, and answers that it wrote to disk at this absolute path with no branch and no merge. No write is refused, and the answer is the safety: it names the checkout, which is what the agent lacked.

Step 1, `seon.repl/edit!` takes any path. Generalize what's there: exact text found once, refusals naming candidate lines, `[[old new] ...]`. Add `seon.text/place` and the multimethod. Plain paths only in this step, so it has value alone and touches nothing in the db. Delete the `(if doc? ...)`. Cost: one read and one write per edit, O(the file's size); a 1 MB file is fine. Proofs: ``seon.repl-test/an-edit-of-a-path-keeps-its-mode``, ``seon.repl-test/an-edit-of-a-path-says-where-it-wrote``.

Step 2, one kind end to end: scripts. ``:seon.script/fact``: ``:seon.script/name`` (\"bin/root\": identity, merge key, path), ``:seon.script/says`` (its lead comment), ``:seon.script/usage`` (read from its text), ``:seon.script/text``, ``:seon.script/mode``; `:seon.ctx/pattern` on the schema, a component answering hiccup that says what it's for and how to call it. An allow-list key maps patterns to kinds: ``:seon.config.source/things`` {\"bin/*\" :seon.script}, documented in `resources/seon/config/default.edn`, read by the import, `seon.root/import-of`, which already skips an unchanged tree. Add the key to `seon.merge/three-way`'s program keys: both sides changing a script is an unmerged entry, shown as a form is. Whole text first; a line merge only if conflicts prove common. `edit!` of a script goes to the branch. The manual sync writes text and mode. A proof that merges it end to end: edit on a branch, merge, sync, `git diff` empty against main, execute bit kept. Do this on a real worktree root by `bin/mcp`, reading what comes back. Then run task `seon.root/roots-log-is-bounded`'s `bin/root` half this way.

Depends on the manual sync: `jolt/audit-fixes` makes write-back a call, with the automatic one behind a switch; the owner's call is to turn automatic off. Don't build a second sync.

The new case the manual sync makes possible: main holds a merged change not yet synced, and a person edits the same file on disk; the next import must neither overwrite nor lose either. It's an unmerged entry. Proof: ``seon.source-test/an-import-beside-an-unsynced-merge-is-an-unmerged-entry``.

Step 3, cites. A script's text goes through `seon.ref/parse`, as a plan, a section and a docstring do, so it cites vars, keys and other scripts by the one parser, and ``:seon.cite/script`` lets a plan's `bin/root` show on the page at its depth. Text search takes ``:seon.script/text``. No file-specific parser.

Step 4, config entries, only after a real need shows it (roots-log's key is one): ``:seon.config/entry-fact``, one entity per entry of default.edn like `:schema/key`, with ``:seon.config/why`` (its comment), its value, a ref to its schema. Two agents adding different keys don't conflict. Note the boot order: bin/root and default.edn are read before a db opens, so a merged change takes effect after sync and restart; an agent reads config through its branch's db so its own new key works at its REPL (this is what `roots-log`'s agent lacked: `:seon.config.root/log-bytes unknown-key`). Make `:schema/file` a ref in that pass.

Later, each when something asks: system.edn as ``:seon.proc``, input.css as ``:seon.style``; ``:seon.test/files``, a test's reads of these things, written when it runs (the per-test read tracking that takes the 98 whole-tree tests down: Optimization holds the test system).

Won't do: worktrees, isolated trees, refused writes, a virtual filesystem, a directory entity, importing anything not on the allow-list, automatic sync. Stop and tell the owner if a kind needs a special case in `edit!`, the merge or the sync: a special case means the wrong design."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/an-edit-of-a-path-keeps-its-mode seon.repl-test/an-edit-of-a-path-says-where-it-wrote seon.repl-test/a-script-is-edited-on-the-branch seon.merge-test/a-script-merges-per-key seon.source-test/a-script-merged-is-written-back-with-its-mode seon.source-test/a-script-says-what-its-for-and-how-to-call-it seon.fx-test/a-file-is-read-as-its-reader-reads-it-before-a-commit}}
