 {:seon.task/name "a-worktree-name-taken-by-an-old-branch-is-refused"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-08: bin/worktree NAME on a name whose branch jolt/NAME already exists, from an older session, checks that branch out and merges main into it, so the new worktree carries the old branch's commits and a session believes it started from main. Seen when an audit's fixes started on jolt/audit-fixes, an old branch whose work was already on main: a revert then conflicted with code main no longer held, and an evals-max line looked like a live bug on main that was not. Correct: refuse a name whose branch exists unless asked to continue it, saying which commit it holds and whether main already has it. Proof: a branch made, then bin/worktree on its name refused, naming it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/bin-worktree-name-name-branch-jolt-name-already}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-task-changed-in-the-db-is-written-back-to-its-file"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-09: \"From that point on we are syncing data TO files not reading from them\"; \"I want seon agents writing updates inside the system and pushing them to disk\" (docs/decisions.md#a-running-system-runs-off-the-db-boot-resumes-from-it-and-reads-no-file-owner-2026-11-09). What exists: a merge writes main's changed namespaces, schema resources, allow-list files and wiki pages back to their files through the one write-back (seon.reconcile's, seon.doc/files over seon.source/files, committed by seon.fx/git-commit on the merges branch: docs/source.md#write-back-mains-files-byte-for-byte). Task files have none: a task is an entity on main (seon.task/load-tx makes it from resources/seon/tasks/<ns>.edn, keeping each declaration's text as :seon.task/declared), and a task an agent creates, edits or completes on its branch stays in the db, so the file and main disagree until someone edits the file by hand, and a seed or a re-index of that file undoes the db's change. Correct: a task's declaration is drawn from main as its file holds it (one file per owning namespace, each declaration as seon.task/file-declarations reads it back, byte for byte for an unchanged file), and the one write-back writes the task files a merge changed beside its code and pages; no second write-back, no file watcher. Proofs, red first: a task file indexed then drawn from main is a zero diff (as seon.source-test/import-then-write-back-is-a-zero-diff); a merge whose branch changed a task's plan writes that file and no other; a task created on a branch appears in its owning namespace's file after the merge."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/task-file-indexed-then-drawn-main-zero-diff}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-test-runners-kept-programs-are-keyed-by-their-indexing-code"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the review of the build's kept index (2026-11-09): test-runner/program-db keeps one branch per git tree in the program store (:seon.config.proofs/program-store), brought to a tree with seon.source/refresh-tx from the nearest kept one, and keys those branches by `importer`, the digest of the whole text of src/seon/source.clj and src/seon/program.clj. That key is coarse (any edit of either file imports every tree again) and misses the code they call outside those files (the reader, seon.reader; Jolt's analyzer, by the runtime pins), so a kept branch can hold what other code made of a file: the class seon.root/kept-index! closes for the build's index and the process store (docs/source.md#the-binary-records-its-program). Correct: the branches are keyed by seon.root/index-code over the kept program (the reach of seon.root/index-roots, with the runtime pins), read and compared as kept-index! does, and `importer` is deleted. Proof, red first: a kept tree indexed under one reach digest, then the reach moved with the tree's files unchanged, plans a fresh import. Merged from seon.program/a-kept-tree-is-keyed-by-the-text-of-two-files (2026-11-10 triage): same cause (the kept tree keyed by the text of source.clj and program.clj); that task gains the measurements and the done rule (a comment edit and a digest-only edit to seon.program each plan in under 300 M ticks and match a whole import datom by datom), and importer and builder-id become one key"
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/kept-tree-indexed-under-reach-digest-then-reach}}
 {:seon.task/name "privacy-comes-from-the-accounts"
  :seon.task/plan "Owed by jolt/index-arg (landed aa08b56b21, which completed seon.source analysis-never-writes-the-image): seon.source/tree-private answers a var's privacy for the analysis env's :private? by reading each changed namespace's forms a second time (text-privacy, recognizing defn- and ^:private by the form's head). read-nss already analyzes the files in load order and each account carries the definition's meta, so the privacy of a namespace analyzed earlier can come from its accounts, and text-privacy goes. The edge to state: a file naming a var of a later file in load order without requiring it. Estimate: index-arg was estimated at +10 to +15 src lines and measured +79 (ix threaded through readers' signatures); this one should delete about 10. Also owed from it, Web's in 2a: the :own-index? flag goes when every walk is handed its index."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/analysis-env-s-private-var-earlier-analyzed-namespace}}]
